{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"**Conceptual examples:** Cross-subsidization occurs when one class bears costs attributable to another. Mutual-fund cash redemptions can generate trading costs and taxable gains borne partly by ETF shareholders; liquidity buffers can create shared cash drag. Conversely, ETF creation/redemption costs not recovered through transaction fees can burden mutual-fund shareholders. ETF in-kind redemptions can also remove low-basis securities, benefiting mutual-fund shareholders through reduced future gain distributions. A shared benefit is not automatically an improper subsidy.\n\n**Representative, order-specific monitoring framework:** Assess each class’s attributable activity and resulting:\n- **Transaction costs:** trading expenses, market impact, and transaction-fee recovery.\n- **Cash:** balances and liquidity needs associated with each class’s flows, including performance effects.\n- **Gains:** realized gains, in-kind removal of embedded gains, and distribution consequences across classes.\n\nApplicable exemptive orders govern monitoring, board reporting, and corrective procedures; these are not universal numerical thresholds. Evaluate burdens and benefits together, not isolated metrics."}}
